import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Retro — marking the real minutes-scale tail slow","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Retro — marking the real minutes-scale tail `slow`","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/slow-marking.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/slow-marking.md"}'),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/slow-marking.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-—-marking-the-real-minutes-scale-tail-slow" tabindex="-1">Retro — marking the real minutes-scale tail <code>slow</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-—-marking-the-real-minutes-scale-tail-slow" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro — marking the real minutes-scale tail slow”">​</a></h1><p>Context: <code>AGENTS.md</code> advertises <code>-m &quot;not slow&quot;</code> as the iteration loop, and justified it with a slice measurement (the <code>test_torch_integration</code> + <code>test_om_decayed_cross</code> slice: 370.8 s → 42.6 s). That is true <em>for that slice</em> and misleading for the suite: only <strong>six</strong> tests carried <code>@pytest.mark.slow</code>, and the suite&#39;s dominant tail was <strong>unmarked</strong>. A serial run stalled at ~36% inside the discovery block; <code>-n 2</code> overlapped the stall and reached 98%. So <code>-m &quot;not slow&quot;</code> did not actually give a fast loop. This retro records the measurement and the marking.</p><h2 id="the-tail-measured-this-host-2026-10-15" tabindex="-1">The tail, measured (this host, 2026-10-15) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-tail-measured-this-host-2026-10-15" aria-label="Permalink to “The tail, measured (this host, 2026-10-15)”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>target</th><th>cost</th><th>how measured</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>test_discovery_open_learned.py</code> (module)</td><td><strong>&gt;900 s</strong> (killed at the box)</td><td>whole module; the module-scoped <code>measured</code> fixture dominates</td></tr><tr><td><code>test_discovery_open_prior.py</code> (module)</td><td><strong>&gt;712 s</strong> (11:52, terminated)</td><td><code>TestPreset</code> passed in seconds, then the <code>measured</code> fixture stalled past the 712-s mark</td></tr><tr><td><code>test_square_rsqrt_laws.py::test_every_miss_spelling_reaches_rms_norm</code></td><td><strong>&gt;9:49</strong>, still climbing (~1.2 GB RSS)</td><td>killed at the 1000-s box</td></tr><tr><td><code>test_square_rsqrt_laws.py::test_canonical_rsqrt_spellings_fold_with_their_bridges</code></td><td><strong>&gt;7 min</strong> (420-s box)</td><td>killed at the box</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The two discovery fixtures are the same shape: a 36-page × 4-arm episode loop over <code>proposal.evaluate</code> (the learned module also trains one epoch from the cold start). The two <code>test_square_rsqrt_laws</code> saturations run <code>EGraph.run(..., max_nodes=60_000)</code> over <em>every</em> recorded-miss spelling of the manual RMSNorm — the canon laws expand the graph to the node cap, which is the minutes.</p><h2 id="measured-fast-so-deliberately-not-marked" tabindex="-1">Measured <strong>fast</strong>, so deliberately not marked <a class="header-anchor" href="#measured-fast-so-deliberately-not-marked" aria-label="Permalink to “Measured fast, so deliberately not marked”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>test_discovery_meta_prior.py</code> — its <code>measured</code> fixture is 0.6 s (a held-out eval, no training).</li><li><code>test_discovery_open_game.py</code> — the whole module is 8.4 s (max single test 2.0 s).</li><li><code>test_square_rsqrt_laws.py::test_real_export_spellings_fold_and_verify</code> — 2.8 s, and <code>::test_bridges_fire_in_default_pipeline</code> — 0.9 s. Both use <code>max_nodes=60_000</code>/<code>max_enodes=100_000</code> but only on the canonical / real-export spellings, which fold before the graph explodes.</li><li>the other module-scoped-fixture files (<code>test_regime</code>, <code>test_regime_edges</code>, <code>test_discovery_coherence*</code>, <code>test_discovery_impact</code>, <code>test_discovery_intake</code>, <code>test_contraction_policy_artifact</code>) — 229 tests in 21.7 s.</li><li>the tens-of-seconds torch tests (<code>test_om_decayed_kway_deliver</code> 20.9/15.8 s, <code>test_om_mha</code> 12.1 s, <code>test_omd_lower</code> 11.7 s) — kept unmarked; they are not minutes-scale.</li></ul><h2 id="the-fixture-caveat-the-thing-to-get-right" tabindex="-1">The fixture caveat (the thing to get right) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-fixture-caveat-the-thing-to-get-right" aria-label="Permalink to “The fixture caveat (the thing to get right)”">​</a></h2><p>A <strong>module-scoped fixture is not skipped by marking one test that uses it.</strong> So the discovery markers go on the <em>classes</em> that request <code>measured</code> (<code>TestHeldOutYield</code>, <code>TestOrderingSensitivity</code>, <code>TestRanking</code> in the learned module; <code>TestHeldOutYield</code>, <code>TestRanking</code> in the prior module) — every test that touches the fixture. With all of them deselected pytest never instantiates the fixture, because fixtures are lazy.</p><p><strong>Verified.</strong> <code>-m &quot;not slow&quot;</code> over the five affected files selects <strong>40 of 64</strong> and runs in <strong>14.1 s</strong>, and no <code>setup</code> entry for either <code>measured</code> fixture appears in <code>--durations</code>. (The same five files without the marker run the learned module alone past 900 s.)</p><h2 id="result" tabindex="-1">Result <a class="header-anchor" href="#result" aria-label="Permalink to “Result”">​</a></h2><p><code>slow</code> now covers <strong>30</strong> tests (the six <code>torch.compile</code> ones + 24 new). <code>AGENTS.md</code>&#39;s <code>-m &quot;not slow&quot;</code> paragraph now names what it skips, what it still costs (the tens-of-seconds tests above), and the fixture caveat.</p><p><strong>Note for the next reader:</strong> these are minutes-scale <em>by design</em>, not by accident — an episode loop over a real evaluation, and an e-graph saturation run to the node cap. They are regression pins for the project&#39;s central claims (the learned arm beats random on held-out pages; the miss spellings fold under <code>ALL_RULES</code>). Do not shrink them to make the loop faster; the marker is the right lever.</p>`,13)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};